\subsection{Archived Cross-Model Scores and Reference-Format Diagnostics}
Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports lexical scores recomputed from the archived text pairs, and Figure~\ref{fig:model_ci} displays the mean-score intervals. Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct, Phi-1.5, Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct, and Gemma-2-2B-it had mean $\phi$ from 0.5170 to 0.5680 and $\mathrm{LR}@0.5$ from 59.67\% to 76.33\%. Phi-1.5 is a base-model stress test; these observations do not isolate a base-versus-instruction effect. ROUGE-1 precision for these five models was 0.2329--0.3149. No threshold for readable paraphrase was calibrated, so these values describe lexical overlap without establishing semantic fidelity.

TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat (0.0302) and Phi-3.5-mini-instruct (0.0434) had no sample with $\phi>0.5$, but their references lack ASCII spaces in 299/300 and 162/300 cases, respectively. Because the term-frequency metric depends on word boundaries and the reference detokenization is unvalidated, these two rows are apparatus diagnostics, not evidence of poor transfer on ordinary responses. The remaining five rows also retain the collector-provenance limitation in Section~\ref{sec:measurement-audit}; their score differences are conditional on the archived pipeline.

