\subsection{Reconstruction from Validated Archived Lengths}
Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports the completed reconstruction cohort, and Figure~\ref{fig:model_ci} shows its mean-score intervals. For the five series without the observed malformed-word-boundary pattern, mean $\phi$ ranges from 0.4820 to 0.5355 and $\mathrm{LR}@0.5$ from 42.33\% to 65.33\%. Their ROUGE-1 precision is 0.2101--0.2921. These metrics quantify lexical overlap with the archived emitted text; they do not establish semantic fidelity or independently validate its historical full-sequence detokenization. Phi-1.5 remains a base-model stress test.

The archived TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini references yield means of 0.0792 and 0.1210, with no $\phi>0.5$ cases. These are reference-format diagnostics. The new input vectors are verified byte-for-byte for all models, but reference defects remain part of these two scores. Across the 2100 reconstructions, all 62,757 available heuristic segments were processed; 1475 encoder calls exceeded the 80-token input limit. No run stopped early because of an empty middle-segment output.

