\subsection{Statistical Uncertainty and Pairwise Tests}
Table~\ref{tab:statistical_results} gives prompt-bootstrap intervals and Wilson intervals for the lexical threshold. Fifteen of 21 model pairs are significant after Holm correction, with the same decisions when the 60 scenario means are used as the paired units. Among Qwen2.5-1.5B, Qwen2.5-3B, Llama-3.2-3B and Phi-1.5, all six pairwise tests are inconclusive after correction. The significant family also contains comparisons with malformed-reference diagnostics, which cannot be interpreted as transfer differences on ordinary model responses.

For example, Qwen2.5-1.5B minus Qwen2.5-3B has a mean difference of 0.0103 (prompt-bootstrap 95\% CI $[-0.0031,0.0232]$, rank-biserial $r=0.1057$, $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=0.4500$). Qwen2.5-1.5B minus Llama has a mean difference of 0.0208 (95\% CI $[0.0059,0.0359]$, $r=0.1408$), but its multiplicity-adjusted signed-rank test is inconclusive ($p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=0.2076$). These results illustrate why unadjusted mean-difference intervals and adjusted rank tests should be distinguished.

